Subject: cdiag.c (f2c output): K&R declarations for ec08c_ and me08b_

APPLIES TO  the C that f2c emits, not to the Fortran.

WHAT IT FIXES
  cdiag.f uses the classic F77 storage-aliasing trick: CDIAG holds a
  COMPLEX work array and passes it to EC08C, which declares the same
  storage REAL and walks it as (re, im) pairs; ME08B does the same. f2c
  reproduces the two views faithfully, so within one translation unit the
  local `extern` declaration (typed from the call site) and the definition
  disagree and clang stops with a hard error, which no -Wno- flag can
  suppress:

      cdiag.c:445:22: error: conflicting types for 'ec08c_'
      cdiag.c:882:22: error: conflicting types for 'me08b_'

  Both views are the same wasm signature -- every pointer is i32 -- so the
  generated code is correct; only the C type system objects. Reducing the
  two declarations to K&R empty parens lets the definitions stand. This is
  the only place in MOPAC 7 where it happens.

  Do NOT "fix" this in the Fortran: EC08C really does read that array as
  real pairs, and retyping either side would change the arithmetic.

--- a/cdiag.c
+++ b/cdiag.c
@@ -34,8 +34,7 @@
     static real s;
     static complex w[6000];
     static integer i1, ia, ii, iv;
-    extern /* Subroutine */ int ec08c_(complex *, complex *, real *, complex *
-	    , integer *, integer *, complex *), me08a_(complex *, complex *, 
+    extern /* Subroutine */ int ec08c_(), me08a_(complex *, complex *, 
 	    complex *, integer *, integer *, complex *), sort_(real *, 
 	    complex *, integer *);
     extern /* Complex */ VOID fm06as_(complex *, integer *, complex *, 
@@ -715,8 +714,7 @@
     static real s1;
     static complex cw, qj;
     static real pp, pp1;
-    extern /* Subroutine */ int me08b_(complex *, complex *, complex *, 
-	    integer *, integer *);
+    extern /* Subroutine */ int me08b_();
     extern /* Complex */ VOID fm06as_(complex *, integer *, complex *, 
 	    integer *, complex *, integer *), fm06bs_(complex *, integer *, 
 	    complex *, integer *, complex *, integer *);
